# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/xERC20/contracts/XERC20.sol#L89-L98


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`XERC20::mint()` dev comments for the function state that the function 
>Can only be called by a bridge

However `XERC20::mint()` has no access control, and when following to `XERC20::_mintWithCaller()` it can be seen that anyone with minting allowance can call `mint()` successfully.

This deviates from the expected behaviour from the comments on the function. It can also be seen within the repo that `RestakeManager::deposit()` also calls this function for staking, which is not a bridge.

## Proof of Concept

[XERC20::mint()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/xERC20/contracts/XERC20.sol#L89-L98)
```solidity
    /**
     * @notice Mints tokens for a user
     * @dev Can only be called by a bridge
     * @param _user The address of the user who needs tokens minted
     * @param _amount The amount of tokens being minted
     */

    function mint(address _user, uint256 _amount) public virtual { // @audit callable by anyone
        _mintWithCaller(msg.sender, _user, _amount);
    }
```
The function states only a bridge can call, however `_mintWithCaller()` has a check to see if bridge, is not bridge then authorised minters can also call using their minting allowance (RestakeManager, etc).

[XERC20::_mintWithCaller()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/xERC20/contracts/XERC20.sol#L348-L358)
```solidity
    function _mintWithCaller(address _caller, address _user, uint256 _amount) internal {
        // Do not allow 0 value mints
        if (_amount == 0) revert IXERC20_INVALID_0_VALUE();

        if (_caller != lockbox) {
            uint256 _currentLimit = mintingCurrentLimitOf(_caller);
            if (_currentLimit < _amount) revert IXERC20_NotHighEnoughLimits();
            _useMinterLimits(_caller, _amount);
        }
        _mint(_user, _amount);
    }
```
This shows that the current code implementation deviates from the intended logic from the code comments.

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Update the comments for the `XERC20::mint()` function to make it clear that a bridge or authorised minting addresses are able to call `mint()` successfully. This is important to provide clarity to users and developers.

If only the bridge is intended to call this function, then change the access control on mint to ensure that the caller is the intended bridge









## Assessed type

Access Control

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L2/Oracle/RenzoOracleL2.sol#L46-L57


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`RenzoOracleL2::getMintRate()` retrieves the price of `ezETH` in `ETH`, however if the price of `ezETH` is less than `1 ETH` the function will revert. The price of `1 ezETH` can be less than `1 ETH` due to market forces. 

At the time of writing, the [chainlink ezeth-eth](https://data.chain.link/feeds/ethereum/mainnet/ezeth-eth) oracle states that `1 ezETH` is worth `0.9868 Ether`. Meaning in the current code, the function would revert incorrectly.

## Proof of Concept
[RenzoOracleL2::getMintRate()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L2/Oracle/RenzoOracleL2.sol#L46-L57)
```solidity
    /**
     * @notice Pulls the price of ezETH
     * @dev reverts if price is less than 1 Ether
     */
    function getMintRate() public view returns (uint256, uint256) {
        (, int256 price, , uint256 timestamp, ) = oracle.latestRoundData();
        if (timestamp < block.timestamp - MAX_TIME_WINDOW) revert OraclePriceExpired();
        // scale the price to have 18 decimals
        uint256 _scaledPrice = (uint256(price)) * 10 ** (18 - oracle.decimals());
        if (_scaledPrice < 1 ether) revert InvalidOraclePrice();
        return (_scaledPrice, timestamp);
    }
```
Currently `getMintRate()` will revert if _`scaledPrice` is less than 1 ether, this is because it is assumed that `ezETH` should always be worth more than 1 ether as it accumulates rewards.

However it is possible for the actual rate of `ezETH` to be less than `ETH` due to issues with the protocol, bad press, long redemption queues, etc 

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Change the check to check for 0 price to avoid erronoues responses. If the protocol is worried about erroneous prices from the oracle, consider using 2 oracles and averaging the price.







