# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L1/xRenzoBridge.sol#L294-L295
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L1/xRenzoBridge.sol#L305-L306


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In the contraxt xRenzoBridge.sol, the `recoverNative` and `recoverERC20` functions do not perform proper validation of the receiver address (`_to`). 

If `_to` is set to a zero address (`0x0`), the tokens or Ether will be lost because they are sent to an address from which recovery is impossible (burning funds)

Impact: Lost of funds for users
## Proof of Concept
Affected code snippets from `recoverNative` and `recoverERC20` functions:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L1/xRenzoBridge.sol#L294-L295

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L1/xRenzoBridge.sol#L305-L306


```solidity
    function recoverNative(uint256 _amount, address _to) external onlyBridgeAdmin {
        payable(_to).transfer(_amount);
    }

    function recoverERC20(address _token, uint256 _amount, address _to) external onlyBridgeAdmin {
        IERC20(_token).safeTransfer(_to, _amount);
    }
```


## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

These functions should check if `_to` is not a zero address before executing the transfer of assets. This could be achieved by adding the following line at the beginning of each function:

```solidity
require(_to != address(0), "Invalid receiver address");
```
After the change, the updated code might look like this:

```solidity
    function recoverNative(uint256 _amount, address _to) external onlyBridgeAdmin {
        require(_to != address(0), "Invalid receiver address");
        payable(_to).transfer(_amount);
    }

    function recoverERC20(address _token, uint256 _amount, address _to) external onlyBridgeAdmin {
        require(_to != address(0), "Invalid receiver address");
        IERC20(_token).safeTransfer(_to, _amount);
    }
```
The contract should prevent asset transfer to a zero address to prevent accidental losses. Executes should avoid transferring funds to an address from which recovery is impossible.





