Invalid Validation

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L2/xRenzoDeposit.sol#L104-L116


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The contract assumes that all tokens interfaced with have 18 decimal places. However, it does not enforce this expectation thoroughly. While the contract does check that the tokens involved in the initialization process (`initialize` function) comply with this assumption, it doesn't enforce this check elsewhere (e.g., when dealing with tokens received in other functions).

Although this assumption might be considered reasonable within the Ethereum ecosystem since most tokens use 18 decimal places, there are execptions such as USDT that only have 6. If a token with mismatched decimal places is used with this contract, unexpected behavior such as incorrect token balances and transactions could occur.

Ether is also explicitly dealt with within the contract using a value of ether (`32 ether` in `initialize()` and comparing against `32 ether` when updating the sweep batch size). Therefore, incorrect behavior could occur in the contract's ether manipulation logic because Ether exists natively on the Ethereum chain and automatically assumes 18 decimal places.

## Proof of Concept
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L2/xRenzoDeposit.sol#L104-L116

In the `initialize` function:

```solidity
// Verify all tokens have 18 decimals
uint8 decimals = IERC20MetadataUpgradeable(address(_depositToken)).decimals();
if (decimals != EXPECTED_DECIMALS) {
    revert InvalidTokenDecimals(EXPECTED_DECIMALS, decimals);
}
decimals = IERC20MetadataUpgradeable(address(_collateralToken)).decimals();
if (decimals != EXPECTED_DECIMALS) {
    revert InvalidTokenDecimals(EXPECTED_DECIMALS, decimals);
}
decimals = IERC20MetadataUpgradeable(address(_xezETH)).decimals();
if (decimals != EXPECTED_DECIMALS) {
    revert InvalidTokenDecimals(EXPECTED_DECIMALS, decimals);
}

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

To mitigate this issue, the contract should be revised to work with variable decimal tokens. Alternatively, the contract can restrict interaction to only tokens that have 18 decimal places. This check should be enforced in all relevant points of interaction not just during initialization.

Developers should also consider making a clear and explicit warning about this issue in the contract's comments or documentation.


## Assessed type

Decimal

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L2/Oracle/RenzoOracleL2.sol#L54


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The function `getMintRate` reads the price from an oracle and scales it to a value with 18 decimal places. This operation to adjust the decimals involves a multiplication of the price with `10 ** (18 - oracle.decimals())`, which could cause numerical overflow if not properly safeguarded.

An overflow occurs when an operation tries to create a number that is outside the maximum limit that can be held by the data type. In Solidity, a `uint256` has a maximum limit of 2^256 - 1, and any calculation that exceeds this value will result in an overflow and the value will loop around to zero.


## Proof of Concept
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L2/Oracle/RenzoOracleL2.sol#L54

```solidity
uint256 _scaledPrice = (uint256(price)) * 10 ** (18 - oracle.decimals());
```

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

In order to prevent this potential overflow issue, a best practice would be to use an open-source library like OpenZeppelin's SafeMath for all arithmetic operations which will throw an error and halt execution when an overflow or underflow condition is encountered. You can see an example on how to integrate SafeMath below:

```solidity
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/utils/math/SafeMath.sol";
...
using SafeMath for uint256;
...

function getMintRate() public view returns (uint256, uint256) {
    (, int256 price, , uint256 timestamp, ) = oracle.latestRoundData();
    if (timestamp < block.timestamp - MAX_TIME_WINDOW) revert OraclePriceExpired();
    // scale the price to have 18 decimals
    uint256 _scaledPrice = (uint256(price)).mul(10 ** (18 - oracle.decimals()));
    if (_scaledPrice < 1 ether) revert InvalidOraclePrice();
    return (_scaledPrice, timestamp);
}
```

By doing so, inside the `getMintRate` method where the scaling occurs, the `*` operator is replaced with `.mul`, effectively making the multiplication operation checked for overflow. Thus, if an overflow condition occurs, it will not be unnoticed, and will revert the transaction, preventing any further potential damage.



## Assessed type

Under/Overflow

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Deposits/DepositQueue.sol#L211-L246


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The contract DepositQueue.sol lacks proper input sanitization in the function `stakeEthFromQueueMulti`, where arrays of input values are used. The contract trusts the caller to provide appropriate inputs, which can lead to unintended behavior if incorrect data is supplied.

In the function `stakeEthFromQueueMulti`, the contract processes multiple calls to `stakeEthInOperatorDelegator` by iterating through the arrays of `operatorDelegators`, `pubkeys`, `signatures`, and `depositDataRoots` and passing each index's values to the stake function. However, it contains no validation to confirm whether the supplied data is appropriate, leading to potential risks if the data is incorrect or malicious.


## Proof of Concept
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Deposits/DepositQueue.sol#L211-L246


'''solidity
function stakeEthFromQueueMulti( IOperatorDelegator[] calldata operatorDelegators, bytes[] calldata pubkeys, bytes[] calldata signatures, bytes32[] calldata depositDataRoots ) external onlyNativeEthRestakeAdmin nonReentrant {
  // Verify all arrays are the same length
  if ( operatorDelegators.length != pubkeys.length || operatorDelegators.length != signatures.length || operatorDelegators.length != depositDataRoots.length )
    revert MismatchedArrayLengths();
  // Iterate through the arrays and stake each one
  uint256 arrayLength = operatorDelegators.length;
  for (uint256 i = 0; i < arrayLength; ) {
    // Send the ETH and the params through to the restake manager
    restakeManager.stakeEthInOperatorDelegator{ value: 32 ether }(
      operatorDelegators[i],
      pubkeys[i],
